.TH MORAINE 1 "2026-09-07" "moraine 0.3.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
moraine \- snapshot backup over SSH/rsync and rclone
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B moraine
.RB [ \-c
.IR CONFIG ]
.I COMMAND
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B moraine
creates timestamped, hard-linked snapshots of local files on a remote target.
Each run looks like a complete tree, but files that have not changed share disk
with the previous snapshot through hard links, so keeping history costs almost
nothing. Snapshots can be listed, pruned with a GFS retention policy, and
individual files or whole trees restored.
.PP
Two backends are supported: rsync over SSH, and rclone (cloud, SFTP, SMB,
WebDAV, S3 and more). Targets and schedules are described in a TOML
configuration file.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " \fICONFIG\fR
Path to the configuration file (default:
.IR moraine.toml ).
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print version, including the git hash and build date.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B init
Write an example configuration file to start from.
.TP
.B recommend \fR[\fB\-\-name\fR \fINAME\fR] [\fB\-\-write\fR]
Look at this machine and suggest what is worth backing up, printed as a ready
.RI [[ target ]]
block. The suggestion is OS-aware: it picks the directories that exist and skips
caches and rebuildable trees. It only prints, unless
.B \-\-write
is given, which appends the block to the configuration file.
.B \-\-name
sets the target name
.RI ( essentials
by default).
.TP
.B verify
Check the SSH connection, key, sources and that the target is writable.
.TP
.B run \fR[\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-\-target\fR \fINAME\fR]
Run a backup for all targets, or a selected one.
.TP
.B list \fB\-\-target\fR \fINAME\fR
List the snapshots on a target.
.TP
.B check \fR[\fB\-\-target\fR \fINAME\fR] [\fB\-\-snapshot\fR \fITIMESTAMP\fR]
Verify a snapshot's contents against the current sources by checksum, and report
how many paths differ. Defaults to the newest snapshot. A difference is normal
for an older snapshot whose sources have changed since, but should be zero
straight after a backup.
.TP
.B prune \fR[\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-\-target\fR \fINAME\fR]
Delete old snapshots according to the target's retention policy.
.TP
.B secrets check \fR[\fB\-\-target\fR \fINAME\fR]
Check that every secret the configuration references can be read right now,
reporting where each comes from without printing any of them. Run it from the
same environment as your scheduled backups: a keyring that works in a desktop
session is usually unreachable from
.BR cron (8).
.TP
.B secrets set \fB\-\-target\fR \fINAME\fR \fR[\fB\-\-field\fR \fIFIELD\fR] [\fB\-\-stdin\fR]
Store one secret in the OS keyring and point the configuration field at it.
.I FIELD
is
.BR password " (default), " crypt_password " or " crypt_salt .
The secret is prompted for without echo, or read from standard input with
.BR \-\-stdin .
.TP
.B secrets migrate \fR[\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-\-target\fR \fINAME\fR]
Move secrets stored as plaintext in the configuration into the OS keyring and
rewrite those fields as
.BR keyring: .
Each secret is written and read back before the configuration is changed.
Fields already pointing at an environment variable or the keyring are left
alone.
.SH SECRETS
Each secret field in
.I moraine.toml
records
.I where
the secret lives, not necessarily the secret itself:
.BR \(dqhunter2\(dq
(in the file),
.BR \(dqenv:VAR\(dq
(an environment variable),
.BR \(dqkeyring:\(dq
(the OS keyring), or
.BR \(dqliteral:...\(dq
for a secret that would otherwise be read as one of those prefixes.
.PP
Keyring support is a build-time feature; without it a
.B keyring:
field fails with an explanatory error rather than resolving to an empty secret.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I moraine.toml
Configuration file: one
.RI [[ target ]]
block per backup destination.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rsync (1),
.BR ssh (1),
.BR rclone (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Jonaz Thern <info@thern.io>
